class ConceptType {
  definition {
    Whether a concept corresponds to a category of objects (general
    concept) or to a single unique object (individual concept).

    Per ISO 704:2022 §5.2-5.3:
      - General concepts depict a potentially unlimited number of
        objects sharing properties. Designations take the form of
        terms (including appellations) or symbols.
      - Individual concepts correspond to a unique object or
        composition considered as a unique object. Designations take
        the form of proper names or symbols.

    Individual concepts occupy the bottom rung of a concept ladder
    and cannot be subdivided further into more specific concepts
    (ISO 704:2022 §5.5.4.2.2). However, an individual concept CAN
    be subdivided into parts via a partitive relation (§5.5.4.3.2).

    Defaults to `general` when omitted. Most terminology work
    concerns general concepts; individual concepts appear in
    domains with proper-name terminology (geography, organizations,
    products, biological taxa sometimes).
  }
  values {
    general
    individual
  }
}
